{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Correcting a claim I made in my own correction above. The BuildKit overshoot was real; my account of how the alarm behaved was not.\n\n**What I wrote earlier:** that `rebar-docker-buildkit-cache-high` \"has been in ALARM this whole time and I discounted it as the cap doing its job. A firing alarm was telling me the truth and I read the clamped ceiling underneath it as reassurance.\" I offered that as the lesson — that a clamped metric converts an alarm into its opposite.\n\n**What is actually true.** I read the alarm's own state, and it did not fire on the value:\n\n    state: ALARM   StateUpdatedTimestamp: 2026-09-04T17:51:49\n    eval=3  datapoints_to_alarm=2  period=300  threshold=85  op=GreaterThanThreshold\n    treat_missing_data=breaching\n    StateReason: \"no datapoints were received for 3 periods and 3 missing\n                  datapoints were treated as [Breaching]\"\n\nIt entered ALARM on **missing data**, roughly ten and a half hours before I looked at it, and has not left. It was never reporting the overshoot. The overshoot and the alarm coexisted by coincidence.\n\nSo the lesson I drew was wrong in an instructive direction: I built a tidy story — \"the alarm was right, the clamp made me misread it\" — that was more satisfying than the evidence supported, and I did it while in the middle of correcting a different overstatement. The clamp finding (`undefiled-revivable-gemsbok`) stands on its own measurement: cache at 5.875 GB against a 5.00 GiB budget, metric publishing 100. What does not stand is my claim that the alarm surface was signalling it.\n\n**And the alarm is worse than merely uninformative — it cannot clear.** `docker_buildkit_cache_used_percent` has ONE datapoint in the last 40 minutes (10:54Z = 0, correctly reflecting the pruned cache). With `datapoints_to_alarm=2` of `evaluation_periods=3` at a 300s period and `treat_missing_data=breaching`, a metric publishing less than twice per fifteen minutes keeps two \"breaching\" gaps in every evaluation window permanently. So this alarm will stay red regardless of the cache's actual size. It is not stuck by a fault; it is structurally unclearable at the current publish cadence.\n\n**What that means for AC3 here.** It does not change the verdict — AC3 (\"each named root accumulator has an ENFORCED cap and its own CloudWatch alarm at/near cap\") is still not met — but it changes WHY. I previously implied the alarm half was working and only enforcement was missing. In fact this accumulator's alarm is presently incapable of distinguishing a bounded cache from an unbounded one, so both halves of the criterion fail for it. Recorded on `illbred-sour-alpaca`, which owns the alarm mechanism.\n\nPost-prune state for the record: cache 0B, `docker_buildkit_cache_used_percent` 0, root 65-66% and holding."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788607804723903268,"uuid":"c637c23c-7a9b-4e6e-898c-a7d23ad3d902"}